SightPress never stops because of a module package. When one can't be used, it is left out: the *Features* screen
lists it under **Features that can’t be used** and `site:module:list` marks it "unavailable", both with the reason,
for example:

- it works with another version of `sightpress/module-interface` than the one installed (update the module, or wait
  for a version that fits);
- it needs another module that isn't installed or can't be used (install that one too);
- its class is missing or fails to start (a broken package: reinstall it, or ask its author).

The reason is also written to the log once.

Every new website starts ready to use:

- six published pages: a home page called "Welcome to" and the website's name, About, Contact (with a contact form
  whose messages go to the first admin), a privacy notice, terms of use and an accessibility statement;
- a main menu with About and Contact, and a footer menu with the other three pages;
- a logo, an icon and a picture for sharing on social media, made from the website's name;
- the roles admin, editor, contributor and member (see [Roles and permissions](../reference/roles-and-permissions.md)).

The ready-made pages are in English, whatever the website's language. Change or translate them in the admin panel.

1. Unzip the file.
2. Open `index.html` in any browser. It works offline: nothing in it loads from the internet, and it needs no server.

The page is the same for every theme, so you can compare one theme with another:

- a top bar with the site name and links to each part of the page;
- a band with the theme's name, its description and a **Download** button;
- one example of every kind of block: headings, text, a button, a picture, a file, two columns, a gallery, a
  recording, a video, and a contact form (it can't send anything: it's a preview);
- the footer.

The zip also holds `screenshot.png` and `screenshot-dark.png`, the top of the page in light and dark mode,
`page.png`, the whole page, and `axe.txt`, the full report of the accessibility check.

Write your review as a comment on the pull request. Whoever reads it may be blind, so say it in words:

- where on the page, and in which mode, for example "The Pictures and files part, in dark mode, at 200% zoom";
- what is wrong, for example "The file link is dark blue on the dark background and hard to read";
- what you expected, for example "The link should be as light as the other links in dark mode".

If you add a screenshot, describe it in the same words. If everything looks right, say so too: "Checked in light
and dark mode at 100% and 200% zoom; looks good" helps as much as finding a problem.

A screenshot helps, but whoever reads your report may be blind. Always add a text description:

- say which screen it is and what you were doing, for example "The Analytics screen, in dark mode, at 200% zoom";
- say what is wrong and where, for example "The legend of the visits chart overlaps the last bar label";
- say what you expected, for example "The legend should be below the chart".

If you can't describe it, say so: we'll ask someone sighted to look.

If you leave out something a command needs, it asks for it, one question at a time, and also asks the optional
questions that have a usual answer. Every command asks the same way:

- a question that ends with "Press Enter for" and a value takes that value when you just press Enter;
- a question that lists choices accepts one of them, in capitals or not;
- a password is not shown as you type it, and is never printed; you type it twice, and if the two are not the same
  you are asked for it again;
- some answers are read back in words for you to confirm, for example a language code;
- a wrong answer is explained and asked again; after 3 wrong answers in a row the command stops and changes
  nothing;
- at the end, the command lists your answers and asks "Is all of this right?". Answer `no` to go through the
  questions again, with your answers kept: press Enter for each one that was right.

If you give everything on the command line, nothing is asked. Add `--no-interaction` (or `-n`) to never be asked:
then a missing or wrong value stops the command with a sentence that says what is wrong.

`<slug>` below is the short name of a website, the one you gave `site:create`, such as `demo`.

Search engines and AI readers are only ever offered pages that are:

- **published** (drafts and pages waiting for review never are);
- **public**;
- **not marked** "Ask search engines and AI search engines not to list this page";
- **not a copy** of a page on another website (a page with an "Address of the original").

While SightPress runs anywhere other than production (the `APP_ENV` setting, for example on your own computer), every
page asks search engines not to list it, and `robots.txt` keeps crawlers out of the whole website.

A module has an id: lower-case words starting with your vendor name. Ours is `acme.events`. Its templates and texts
use a shorter name, `acme_events`.

1. In `composer.json`, change `name` to `acme/events`, write your own `description`, `homepage` and `support`, and
   change the namespace `Acme\\MyModule\\` to `Acme\\Events\\` in `autoload` and `autoload-dev`.
2. In `src/Migrations.php`, change `Acme\MyModule` to `Acme\Events` everywhere it appears.
3. Rename `translations/my_module.en-GB.xlf` to `translations/acme_events.en-GB.xlf`.
4. Delete `src/MyModule.php` and the template's tests, `tests/Unit/MyModuleTest.php` and
   `tests/Integration/InstallTest.php`: you write your own below.

In `composer.json`, replace `extra` with:

```json
"extra": {
    "sightpress": {
        "module": "Acme\\Events\\EventsModule",
        "features": {
            "acme.events": {
                "name": "Events",
                "description": "A page for each event, with its date and time, and an Events screen to add them.",
                "when-off": "Event pages answer “not found”, the Events screen leaves the admin panel and event cards are hidden. Events are kept."
            }
        }
    }
}
```

`module` names the class SightPress creates. Under `features`, the name, description and "when it is off" text are
what site admins read on the **Features** screen, so write them in plain words.

Events live in the site's database, in a table of the module's own. Add the libraries the module uses:

```sh
composer require doctrine/dbal:^4 doctrine/migrations:^3 psr/http-factory:^1
composer require --dev nyholm/psr7:^1
```

A migration creates the table. SightPress runs it when a site turns the module on, and undoes it (with `down()`) when
an admin deletes the module's data. It must work on every database SightPress supports (SQLite, MySQL, MariaDB and
PostgreSQL), so it describes the table rather than writing SQL.

